===================================================================================
/etc/os-release AND /usr/lib/os-release
===================================================================================

1. OVERVIEW
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The `os-release` file is a standard, plain-text configuration file used by modern
Linux operating systems operating under systemd or compliant init systems. It
provides operating system identification data in a simple, shell-compatible
key-value format without executing shell scripts.


2. FILE LOCATIONS AND PRECEDENCE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The system inspects two locations to retrieve OS identification metadata:

    1. /etc/os-release  : Primary configuration path. Used for local overrides or
                      vendor-configured runtime settings.

    2. /usr/lib/os-release : Fallback configuration path. Vendor-supplied read-only
                         default shipped with the OS image/package.

Lookup Hierarchy:
+-------------------------------------------------------------+
| Check /etc/os-release                                       |
|   |--> File exists? -------> [ READ /etc/os-release ]       |
|   `--> File missing?                                        |
|         `--> Fallback ------> [ READ /usr/lib/os-release ]  |
+-------------------------------------------------------------+

* Sysadmin Note: /etc/os-release is often a symlink pointing to 
  /usr/lib/os-release or a distribution-specific file (such as /etc/redhat-release
  or /etc/lsb-release on legacy systems).

3. FORMAT GUIDELINES
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Key-Value Structure : Enforced as KEY=VALUE on separate lines.

- Shell Compatibility : Syntax matches standard bash variable assignments, 
                        allowing safe sourcing in scripts (`source /etc/os-release`).

- Quoting Rules       : Values containing spaces, quotes, or special characters 
                        must be enclosed in double (") or single (') quotes. 
                        Standard backslash escape rules apply (\", \\, \$).

- Comments            : Lines starting with # are treated as comments and ignored.


4. STANDARD VARIABLE DEFINITIONS
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Variable Name   Req/Opt   Description & Example Value
-------------   -------   -----------------------------------------------------
NAME            Required  User-friendly string identifying the OS.
                          Example: "Ubuntu" / "RHEL"

VERSION         Optional  Full version string including release name/patch.
                          Example: "24.04 LTS (Noble Numbat)"

ID              Required  Lowercase, lower-ascii string identifying the OS (no spaces).
                          Example: ubuntu / rhel / debian

ID_LIKE         Optional  Space-separated list of parent/derivative distributions.
                          Example: "rhel fedora" / "debian"

VERSION_ID      Optional  Lowercase, lower-ascii version identifier without spaces.
                          Example: "24.04" / "9.4"

PRETTY_NAME     Required  Full descriptive OS name intended for display/logs.
                          Example: "Ubuntu 24.04 LTS"

ANSI_COLOR      Optional  ANSI color sequence for console output formatting.
                          Example: "0;31" / "1;34"

CPE_NAME        Optional  Common Platform Enumeration (CPE) string (NIST standard).
                          Example: "cpe:/o:canonical:ubuntu_linux:24.04"

HOME_URL        Optional  Official home page URL of the operating system.
                          Example: "https://www.ubuntu.com/"

BUG_REPORT_URL  Optional  Issue tracker or support portal URL.
                          Example: "https://bugs.launchpad.net/ubuntu/"


5. PRODUCTION FILE EXAMPLES
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

--- Example A: Ubuntu 24.04 LTS ---
NAME="Ubuntu"
VERSION="24.04 LTS (Noble Numbat)"
ID=ubuntu
ID_LIKE=debian
PRETTY_NAME="Ubuntu 24.04 LTS"
VERSION_ID="24.04"
HOME_URL="https://www.ubuntu.com/"
SUPPORT_URL="https://help.ubuntu.com/"
BUG_REPORT_URL="https://bugs.launchpad.net/ubuntu/"
PRIVACY_POLICY_URL="https://www.ubuntu.com/legal/terms-and-policies/privacy-policy"
UBUNTU_CODENAME=noble

--- Example B: Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9 ---
NAME="Red Hat Enterprise Linux"
VERSION="9.4 (Plow)"
ID="rhel"
ID_LIKE="fedora"
VERSION_ID="9.4"
PLATFORM_ID="platform:el9"
PRETTY_NAME="Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9.4 (Plow)"
ANSI_COLOR="0;32"
CPE_NAME="cpe:/o:redhat:enterprise_linux:9::baseos"
HOME_URL="https://www.redhat.com/"
BUG_REPORT_URL="https://bugzilla.redhat.com/"


6. SCRIPTING AND USAGE
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Bash Sourcing Example:

    #!/usr/bin/env bash
    set -euo pipefail

    if [[ -f /etc/os-release ]]; then
        source /etc/os-release
    elif [[ -f /usr/lib/os-release ]]; then
        source /usr/lib/os-release
    else
        echo "ERROR: Unable to locate os-release file." >&2
        exit 1
    fi

    echo "Detected OS: ${PRETTY_NAME}"
    echo "Base ID:     ${ID}"

    case "${ID}" in
        ubuntu|debian)
            echo "Running Debian/Ubuntu package routine..."
            ;;
        rhel|centos|rocky|almalinux)
            echo "Running RHEL-family package routine..."
            ;;
        *)
            echo "Unsupported distribution family."
            exit 1
            ;;
    esac

